# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Gate Four Reaches the Note Late

**Story & Context:** At 152 BPM, Lan Chi begins with one natural harmonic pluck and a slow upward RISE toward a suspended fourth. Aditya keeps the opening koplo groove unchanged for two subdivisions after the bend begins, creating the first Rod Delay. Upper percussion then thins while the pitch keeps climbing. The instant HOLD stabilizes, Minh Phúc launches a compact kendang double and Maya strikes one prepared-electric-piano fourth/fifth shell that had been withheld throughout the bend. A short mono-bass root enters only after the apex has been confirmed. The first Return Cut removes snare and hats during YIELD, leaving kick, bass clarinet breath, and the falling đàn bầu tone until RETURN settles.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Irrigation gate diagram marked GATE 4, one curved pitch line reaching a horizontal target after the drum-grid marker, wet mechanical lever beside a water-level scale. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “gate four,” “late to the note,” “hold there,” and “return clean.”

## Track 2: Canal Ledger Withholds the Sixth

**Story & Context:** At 156 BPM, the entire track is organized around Apex Debt. Lan Chi repeatedly bends from a fifth harmonic toward the sixth while Maya and Aditya deliberately avoid that scale degree in piano and bass. Koplo drums remain dense during PLUCK, thin through RISE, then explode into fast kendang alternation once HOLD lands. Dewi adds a low bass-clarinet pedal beneath the bend but never moves with it. Midway through, Lan Chi intentionally overshoots the sixth by a narrow microtonal amount before settling back. The supporting chord waits until the true HOLD point rather than following the overshoot, making physical intonation part of the arrangement logic.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Handwritten canal ledger with the sixth column covered by a brass ruler until one red arrival mark, a đàn bầu pitch curve crossing the page, rain drops staining the paper edge. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “sixth withheld,” “not yet,” “past the mark,” and “now release.”

## Track 3: Sluice Pressure Returns Without the Drum

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 144 BPM and begins with sparse kendang, no mono bass, and long đàn bầu arcs. Lan Chi performs the widest bend on the EP, rising from a low harmonic toward the octave over several bars. Aditya uses almost no snare during RISE and lets Dewi’s chalumeau breath provide the only low-mid motion. At HOLD, the arrangement briefly reaches maximum density: rapid kendang, one clipped kick pattern, prepared-piano add9 shell, and one short bass pulse. The final three minutes progressively lengthen YIELD and RETURN while shortening the active HOLD passages. In the last cycle, Lan Chi releases the rod slowly back toward the original harmonic. Aditya executes the final Return Cut but never restores the expected kick. The EP ends with the string settling, woodblock room reflection, and rain against the control-room window.

